  # reasoning model in early access" whose "prompts and outputs will be retained by the provider", with a
  # 256,000-token context and "tools" and "tool_choice" among its supported parameters. Nothing says who
  # makes it or for how long it stays, so it can leave the free list without notice.
  - model_name: glyph-cluster-kilo
    litellm_params:
      model: openai/stealth/glyph-cluster
      api_base: "https://ai-gateway.kilo.ai/api/v1"
      api_key: "kilo-no-key-needed"
      extra_headers:
        Authorization: ""

  # Served, but not routed to: it answered none of the seven requests made by hand on 2026-11-22, each refused
  # with a 429 "Provider returned error", where the other entries of this section answered. The same model
  # through OpenRouter was set aside above the day before for the same reason. The catalogue lists it free,
  # with a 1,048,576-token context and "tools" and "tool_choice" among its supported parameters. Uncommenting
  # it, behind "glyph-cluster-kilo", is all that putting it back takes.
  # - model_name: ling-3-2-flash-kilo
  #   litellm_params:
  #     model: openai/inclusionai/ling-3.2-flash:free
  #     api_base: "https://ai-gateway.kilo.ai/api/v1"
  #     api_key: "kilo-no-key-needed"
  #     extra_headers:
  #       Authorization: ""

{% if has.Requesty %}
  # ============================================================================
  # Requesty ("openai/" through its OpenAI-compatible endpoint, free models only)
  #
  # Checked 2026-11-22. Requesty is a gateway that passes each request on to the model's own provider. LiteLLM
  # has no provider of that name, so each entry is "openai/<provider>/<model>" with an "api_base": LiteLLM
  # cuts the prefix once, and the rest, slash included, reaches Requesty as the model id, which Requesty asks
  # in the form "provider/model" ("Quickstart" of the Requesty docs, read that day; a bare name is a managed
  # policy and is never used here). The base is the European one, which the "Data privacy" page says
  # processes and stores traffic in Frankfurt. That the free models below are all served there is UNVERIFIED:
  # the United States base, one line per entry, is the change to make if one answers "not found".
  #
  # The "Free models" page of the Requesty docs (read that day) limits free usage per organization: 200
  # requests a day and 20 a minute for a new one, 1,000 and 60 for one that pays, "across all free models
  # combined", reset daily. That is a pool of its own, apart from OpenRouter's, and every entry below draws on
  # it together, so a slot here is a second quota only as long as the pool lasts. The same page says the models
  # are free "for now" and that a change will be announced in the changelog ahead of time: the entries below
  # stop being free the day that happens, and 402 is what the "Billing" page says a paid model answers at a
  # zero balance. The "Data privacy" page says Requesty does not store prompts and responses unless logging is
  # enabled and does not train on them, and that what the upstream provider keeps depends on that provider.
  # One account only. The session that wrote this did not read Requesty's terms.
  #
  # Only second sources of an OpenRouter ":free" entry, each right behind its twin. Whether the models are
  # the same weights behind both gateways rests on the names alone. Looked at the same day and not routed,
  # since none is a model this list serves through another provider: Nemotron 3 Super 120B and Nano 30B,
  # Nemotron 3.5 content safety, Muse Glimmer, Laguna, Ling and Leanstral, all on that page. The Gemma 4 26B
  # and the Nemotron 3.5 Super VL of the OpenRouter section have no counterpart on it. Tool calls through
  # these entries are untried: LiteLLM forwards them, and no page read says each model takes them.
  # ============================================================================
  - model_name: nemotron-ultra-requesty
    litellm_params:
      model: openai/nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b
      api_base: "https://router.eu.requesty.ai/v1"
      api_key: "os.environ/REQUESTY_API_KEY"

  - model_name: nemotron-reasoning-requesty
    litellm_params:
      model: openai/nvidia/nemotron-3-nano-omni-30b-a3b-reasoning
      api_base: "https://router.eu.requesty.ai/v1"
      api_key: "os.environ/REQUESTY_API_KEY"

  - model_name: nemotron-lightning-requesty
    litellm_params:
      model: openai/nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning-30b-a3b
      api_base: "https://router.eu.requesty.ai/v1"
      api_key: "os.environ/REQUESTY_API_KEY"

  - model_name: gemma-4-31b-requesty
    litellm_params:
      model: openai/google/gemma-4-31b-it
      api_base: "https://router.eu.requesty.ai/v1"
      api_key: "os.environ/REQUESTY_API_KEY"

{% endif %}

  # ============================================================================
  # OmniRoute -- local, last-resort, deliberately keyless (see the note at the top of this file).
  # Reachable by every account: there is no quota behind it to isolate.
  # ============================================================================
  - model_name: omniroute-free-auto
    litellm_params:
      model: openai/auto/best-free
      api_base: "http://omniroute:20128/v1"
      api_key: "omniroute-no-key-needed"

  - model_name: omniroute-free-code
    litellm_params:
      model: openai/auto/best-coding
      api_base: "http://omniroute:20128/v1"
      api_key: "omniroute-no-key-needed"

  - model_name: omniroute-free-reasoning
    litellm_params:
      model: openai/auto/best-reasoning
      api_base: "http://omniroute:20128/v1"
      api_key: "omniroute-no-key-needed"

router_settings:
  # A member that fails is not retried: the cascade below is the retry. Each retry was paid in full by
  # every member, and the head of a cascade is both what its alias points at and the first slot of its
  # list, so a head that answered 429 or stayed silent used up several attempts, and a dead head cost
  # that again on every request, since a cooldown is not kept for a member reached through an alias.
  num_retries: 0
  retry_after: 2
  cooldown_time: 60
  allowed_fails: 1
  # How long a member may stay silent before the cascade moves on to the next one. They belong here and
  # not in "litellm_settings", where "stream_timeout" has no effect on the router. 90 s for a stream
  # because a model that reasons before it answers is silent until its first answer token, which the
  # slowest of our members reaches after about a minute on average; a stream whose first chunk has not
  # come by then is given up.
  timeout: 120
  stream_timeout: 90

  # Shares cooldown state between the "--num_workers" processes below: without it, each worker tracks
  # its own 429s independently, and a deployment can be considered healthy by one worker while another
  # has already cooled it down (#71).
  redis_host: "redis"
  redis_port: 6379
  redis_password: os.environ/REDIS_PASSWORD

  # Every cascade below stays inside the one account's keys, end to end -- there is only one account
  # in this file for v1 (see the note at the top), so there is nothing yet for a cascade to cross.
  # "check_LiteLLM_account_isolation.py" (#32) is what keeps that true once a second account's models
  # land here.
  fallbacks:
    # Ordering rule inside every cascade below: rank by capability first, most capable to least --
    # general-intelligence leaderboards (LMArena, Artificial Analysis) for "pro"/"flash"/"flash-lite",
    # coding-specific leaderboards (SWE-bench Pro, Aider polyglot, LiveCodeBench) for "code" and
    # "claude-code" -- with speed only a secondary tie-breaker and cost barely entering the comparison
    # at all, since almost every entry here is free. A model's ELIGIBILITY for a cascade is a separate
    # question from its rank inside it: it must first fit that cascade's own latency/purpose profile
    # (flash-lite is for atomic sub-300ms tasks, pro is for unbounded reasoning, claude-code
    # additionally requires confirmed structured tool/function-calling support) before its capability
    # earns it a slot there -- a slow-but-brilliant "pro" model never belongs in flash-lite no matter
    # how far ahead it would rank there on intelligence alone.
    #
    # "gemini-3-8-flash" is the clearest case of the rule in practice: per Artificial Analysis'
    # Intelligence Index (measured 2026-09-13) it sat marginally ahead of Gemini 3.1 Pro despite the
    # "flash" name, so it ranks in the pro cascade below, not just the flash one -- and Gemini 3.1 Pro
    # has since left that cascade altogether, having no free tier (see its model_list comment). Inside
    # "code", the ranking criterion switches to coding benchmarks specifically, which is what put
    # OpenRouter's free GLM 5.2 (SWE-bench Pro 62.1%) at the head of that cascade until its ":free"
    # tier was dropped (#111). Its name stayed there after #111 commented the entry out, pointing at a
    # group that no longer existed; "check_LiteLLM_account_isolation.py" now refuses a fallback to an
    # undeclared name (its rule 5).
    #
    # Cost enters the ranking through Mistral and DeepSeek direct, the two paid providers, which rank
    # after every free entry, Mistral first.

    # 1. PRO -- pure reasoning, multi-step logic, architecture.
    # Requesty's two Nemotron entries sit right behind their OpenRouter twins.
    # Priority: Google -> OpenRouter heavy -> DeepSeek direct (paid -- last resort before the fully
    # free OmniRoute) -> OmniRoute. Kilo's entries sit right behind their OpenRouter twins, ahead of the paid
    # ones; "beam-kilo" has no twin and is ahead of both Nemotron entries, for the reason given with it.
{% if head['pro'] %}
    - pro: &pro_cascade
{% if has.Gemini %}
        - gemini-4-hydrogen
        - gemini-3-8-flash
{% endif %}
        - beam-kilo
{% if has.OpenRouter %}
        - nemotron-ultra-free
{% endif %}
{% if has.Requesty %}
        - nemotron-ultra-requesty
{% endif %}
        - nemotron-ultra-kilo
{% if has.Mistral %}
{% endif %}
{% if has.OpenRouter %}
        - nemotron-reasoning-free
{% endif %}
{% if has.Requesty %}
        - nemotron-reasoning-requesty
{% endif %}
        - nemotron-reasoning-kilo
{% if has.OpenRouter %}
        - nemotron-super-vl-free
{% endif %}
        - nemotron-super-vl-kilo
{% if has.DeepSeek %}
        - deepseek-v4-pro-direct
{% endif %}
        - omniroute-free-reasoning
{% for alias in aliases_of['pro'] %}
    - {{ alias }}: *pro_cascade
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

    # 2. FLASH -- balanced daily driver.
    # Priority: Google Flash (1M) -> LPU (Groq) -> OpenRouter -> Mistral (paid) -> DeepSeek direct
    # (paid -- last resort before the fully free OmniRoute) -> OmniRoute. Entries sit with their
    # twin rather than with their provider: "gemma-4-31b-gemini" (Google) right ahead of
    # "gemma-4-31b-free" -- the same model through a second quota, ranked where the model ranks, and
    # the OpenRouter one second because its quota is the daily count all ":free" entries share.
    # "compound-groq" dropped by #169: decommissioned by Groq 2026-09-21, with no replacement.
    # Requesty's "gemma-4-31b-requesty" sits right behind its OpenRouter twin, a pool of its own.
    # Kilo's entries sit right behind their OpenRouter twins, ahead of the paid ones. The two of its own with no
    # figure, "flint-kilo" and "glyph-cluster-kilo", end the free entries, ahead of OpenRouter's free router,
    # which has no record either and closes the free ones.
    # Cloudflare's "gpt-oss-120b-cloudflare" sits right behind its Groq twin, the same model through an
    # allowance of its own.
{% if head['flash'] %}
    - flash: &flash_cascade
{% if has.Gemini %}
        - gemini-4-hydrogen
        - gemini-3-8-flash
        - gemini-3-6-flash
        - gemini-3-flash-preview
{% endif %}
{% if has.Groq %}
        - gpt-oss-120b-groq
{% endif %}
{% if has.Cloudflare %}
        - gpt-oss-120b-cloudflare
{% endif %}
{% if has.Groq %}
        - qwen3-8-27b-groq
{% endif %}
{% if has.Gemini %}
        - gemma-4-31b-gemini
{% endif %}
{% if has.OpenRouter %}
        - gemma-4-31b-free
{% endif %}
{% if has.Requesty %}
        - gemma-4-31b-requesty
{% endif %}
{% if has.OpenRouter %}
        - particle-1-free
{% endif %}
        - particle-1-kilo
{% if has.OpenRouter %}
        - ernie-5-5-flash-free
{% endif %}
        - ernie-5-5-flash-kilo
        - beam-kilo
{% if has.OpenRouter %}
        - nemotron-super-vl-free
{% endif %}
        - nemotron-super-vl-kilo
        - flint-kilo
        - glyph-cluster-kilo
{% if has.OpenRouter %}
        - openrouter-auto-free
{% endif %}
{% if has.Mistral %}
        - mistral-small
        - ministral-14b
        - ministral-8b
{% endif %}
{% if has.DeepSeek %}
        - deepseek-flash-direct
{% endif %}
        - omniroute-free-auto
{% for alias in aliases_of['flash'] %}
    - {{ alias }}: *flash_cascade
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

    # 3. FLASH-LITE -- atomic micro-tasks (<300ms). Accepts short-context (4k-32k) models for
    # ultra-high speed. "compound-mini-groq" and "qwen3-6-27b-groq" dropped by #169, both retired by
    # Groq in September 2026 and deleted from the model_list; "qwen3-8-27b-groq", Groq's designated
    # successor to the latter, holds its slot -- where the POC ranked its predecessor, until the test
    # bench (#85) measures whether its latency earns it a higher or a lower one. "gemma-4-26b-gemini"
    # sits right ahead of its OpenRouter twin, for the reason given under FLASH, the entries of Requesty and of Kilo, in that order, right
    # behind their OpenRouter twins, and the Cloudflare twins ("gpt-oss-20b-cloudflare", "gemma-4-26b-cloudflare") right
    # behind the last slot of theirs. "llama-3-3-70b-cloudflare" is new to the gateway and closes the free
    # members: no index of Artificial Analysis measures it, and Epoch AI's capabilities index puts it under all
    # of them.
{% if head['flash-lite'] %}
    - flash-lite: &flash_lite_cascade
{% if has.Gemini %}
        - gemini-3-1-flash-lite
        - gemini-2-5-flash-lite
{% endif %}
{% if has.Gemini %}
        - gemini-4-hydrogen
{% endif %}
{% if has.Groq %}
        - gpt-oss-20b-groq
{% endif %}
{% if has.Cloudflare %}
        - gpt-oss-20b-cloudflare
{% endif %}
{% if has.Groq %}
        - qwen3-8-27b-groq
{% endif %}
{% if has.Gemini %}
        - gemma-4-26b-gemini
{% endif %}
{% if has.OpenRouter %}
        - gemma-4-26b-free
{% endif %}
{% if has.Cloudflare %}
        - gemma-4-26b-cloudflare
{% endif %}
{% if has.OpenRouter %}
        - nemotron-lightning-free
{% endif %}
{% if has.Requesty %}
        - nemotron-lightning-requesty
{% endif %}
        - nemotron-lightning-kilo
{% if has.OpenRouter %}
        - lfm-3-1-7b-free
{% endif %}
        - lfm-3-1-7b-kilo
{% if has.Cloudflare %}
        - llama-3-3-70b-cloudflare
{% endif %}
{% if has.Mistral %}
        - ministral-3b
{% endif %}
{% for alias in aliases_of['flash-lite'] %}
    - {{ alias }}: *flash_lite_cascade
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

    # 4. CODE -- refactoring, Ansible playbooks, Bash/Python scripts. Excludes FIM (raw token) models.
    # Ranked by coding-specific benchmarks (SWE-bench Pro, Aider polyglot, LiveCodeBench) rather than
    # general intelligence -- measured 2026-09-13, sources in the model_list comments above. This is
    # why "codestral" -- Mistral's own dedicated code model -- sits near the bottom rather than the
    # top: no current coding-benchmark placement could be found for it (a weak predecessor proxy). Its
    # sibling "mistral-code-agent" is gone from this cascade entirely, not just reordered: #112 found no
    # evidence "mistral-code-agent-latest" was ever a currently published Mistral model id.
    # Priority: syntax/code specialists, Mistral's among them (paid) -> DeepSeek direct (paid -- last
    # resort before the fully free OmniRoute) -> OmniRoute. "glm-4-7-flash-cloudflare" and "glm-4-7-flash-zai"
    # close the free members, ahead of the paid ones: none of the figures this ranking reads measures them.
{% if head['code'] %}
    - code: &code_cascade
{% if has.Gemini %}
        - gemini-4-hydrogen
        - gemini-3-8-flash
{% endif %}
{% if has.Groq %}
        - qwen3-8-27b-groq
{% endif %}
{% if has.Gemini %}
        - gemini-3-6-flash
{% endif %}
{% if has.Cloudflare %}
        - glm-4-7-flash-cloudflare
{% endif %}
{% if has.Zai %}
        - glm-4-7-flash-zai
{% endif %}
{% if has.Mistral %}
        - codestral
        - codestral-2508
{% endif %}
{% if has.OpenRouter %}
{% endif %}
{% if has.DeepSeek %}
        - deepseek-v4-pro-direct
{% endif %}
        - omniroute-free-code
{% for alias in aliases_of['code'] %}
    - {{ alias }}: *code_cascade
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

    # 5. CLAUDE-CODE -- 100% agentic CLI (strict tool/function calling, 128k+ context). It is the "code"
    # cascade, member for member and in the same order, without the members that cannot meet that:
    # "omniroute-free-code" routes to whichever free model OmniRoute picks, and OmniRoute's own reference
    # says a free provider may emulate tool calls from the prompt or drop them, so nothing guarantees
    # either. "check_compose_template.py" holds the two cascades to this. Excludes models with no
    # structured tool support (e.g. mistral-vibe-cli). DeepSeek direct is last: its
    # models support tool calls, but it is the only paid provider in this cascade. This is what #13's
    # Claude Code agent asks for, and whatever it resolves to must never be an Anthropic model backed
    # by a subscription (see its compliance note).
{% if head['claude-code'] %}
    - claude-code: &claude_code_cascade
{% if has.Gemini %}
        - gemini-4-hydrogen
        - gemini-3-8-flash
{% endif %}
{% if has.Groq %}
        - qwen3-8-27b-groq
{% endif %}
{% if has.Gemini %}
        - gemini-3-6-flash
{% endif %}
{% if has.Cloudflare %}
        - glm-4-7-flash-cloudflare
{% endif %}
{% if has.Zai %}
        - glm-4-7-flash-zai
{% endif %}
{% if has.Mistral %}
        - codestral
        - codestral-2508
{% endif %}
{% if has.DeepSeek %}
        - deepseek-v4-pro-direct
{% endif %}
{% for alias in aliases_of['claude-code'] %}
    - {{ alias }}: *claude_code_cascade
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

  # Transparent alias mappings.
  model_group_alias:
    # The routers are written out one by one and not in a loop: "check_LiteLLM_account_isolation.py"
    # reads this file without rendering it, and a name produced by a loop is invisible to it.
{% if head['pro'] %}
    "pro": "{{ head['pro'] }}"
{% endif %}
{% if head['flash'] %}
    "flash": "{{ head['flash'] }}"
{% endif %}
{% if head['flash-lite'] %}
    "flash-lite": "{{ head['flash-lite'] }}"
{% endif %}
{% if head['code'] %}
    "code": "{{ head['code'] }}"
{% endif %}
{% if head['claude-code'] %}
    "claude-code": "{{ head['claude-code'] }}"
{% endif %}
{% for router in ['pro', 'flash', 'flash-lite', 'code', 'claude-code'] if head[router] %}
{% for alias in aliases_of[router] %}
    "{{ alias }}": "{{ head[router] }}"
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}

general_settings:
  # The master key and the database URL are read from the environment rather than written here: this
  # file is mounted read-only into the container and would otherwise carry both in clear text.
  master_key: os.environ/LITELLM_MASTER_KEY
  database_url: os.environ/DATABASE_URL

litellm_settings:
  request_timeout: 120          # long enough for heavy reasoning models' thinking phase
  telemetry: false               # no outbound telemetry
  drop_params: true              # silently drops hyperparameters a given model does not support
  modify_params: true            # translates tool-call schemas between Anthropic and OpenAI/Gemini
  force_ipv4: true              # avoids slow IPv6 resolution on the target VM
  stream_response_threshold: 0   # flushes SSE chunks to the client immediately
  # Keep the gateway's own logging free of prompt and response bodies. The corpus this estate routes
  # includes third-party legal documents, and a debug log is a copy of them nobody decided to keep.
  turn_off_message_logging: true

  # No response cache, on purpose (#79). The two lines that were here, "enable_caching" and a
  # "cache_params" block, meant nothing to LiteLLM, which turns its cache on for "cache: true" and for
  # no other key: it had never been on, and leaving the key out keeps it off.
  #
  # It should stay off. The exact cache's key holds the whole "messages" list and the request's
  # parameters, and nothing about the caller: measured 2026-09-26 on LiteLLM 1.100.0 with two virtual
  # keys, an identical request sent with key B came back from the cache filled by key A, and only the
  # semantic caches add the key, team and organisation. A chat client resends a history that differs
  # almost every turn, so it hardly ever hits; what is left to gain is a workflow repeating one
  # stateless prompt within a short TTL.
